# Accounts: none at first (no login, everyone may do everything); the first
# account is an admin and the console is logged in as it; commands that
# change the system need an admin; passwords are never shown or stored.
# (At boot the console asks like after `logout`: the same code.)
@disk
$ whoami
nobody: this machine has no accounts, so everyone may do everything ('useradd NAME' makes the first, an admin)
$& useradd bart
> beekeeper1
> beekeeper1
$wait
account bart made (admin), home folder /home/bart
the first account: from now on the console asks for a name and password at boot
logged in as bart
!~ beekeeper1
$ whoami
bart (admin)
$& useradd ann
> annpass1
> annpass1
$wait
account ann made, home folder /home/ann
$& useradd tim
> short
$wait
a password needs at least 6 characters
no account made
$ users
~ ^bart +admin$
~ ^ann *$
$ cat /system/users
!~ beekeeper1|annpass1
~ ^bart:admin:[0-9a-f]{16}:[0-9a-f]{64}$
# Ann logs in: she may look, not change the system.
$& logout
> ann
> annpass1
$wait
logged out
welcome, ann
!~ annpass1
$ whoami
ann (no groups)
$ pwd
/home/ann
$ reboot
reboot: only for admins (ann is not one; see whoami)
$ vnc on
vnc: only for admins (ann is not one; see whoami)
$ vnc
remote desktop (VNC): off (vnc on [PORT] starts it)
$ useradd eve
useradd: only for admins (ann is not one; see whoami)
# Files: her home is hers, /system the admins', bart's home bart's; anyone may read.
$ echo hello > /home/ann/a.txt
$ cat /home/ann/a.txt
hello
$ echo shared > /shared.txt
$ echo x > /home/bart/b.txt
/home/bart/b.txt: belongs to bart (anyone may read it, only they may change it)
$ rm /system/users
/system/users: belongs to the admins (anyone may read it, only they may change it)
$ mv /home/ann/a.txt /home/bart/a.txt
/home/bart/a.txt: belongs to bart (anyone may read it, only they may change it)
$ mkdir /home/eve
/home/eve: belongs to eve (anyone may read it, only they may change it)
$ cat /system/users
~ ^bart:admin:
# A wrong password: a pause, and the question again.
$& logout
> bart
> wrong-one
> bart
> beekeeper1
$wait
wrong name or password
welcome, bart
$ echo admins-may > /home/ann/from-bart.txt
$ userdel ann
account ann removed (its home folder stays)
$ userdel bart
account bart removed (its home folder stays); no accounts are left: no login any more
$ whoami
nobody: this machine has no accounts, so everyone may do everything ('useradd NAME' makes the first, an admin)
$ log 30
~ "ev":"login","result":"wrong"
!~ ann|bart|beekeeper
